WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.353
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:06.735
How did you get to MIT?

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.980
I sort of had a long
path to getting here.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.080
But I was a math
undergrad, and then

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.810
I actually was an economics
PhD for a couple of years

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.810
before deciding that
robotics was my true calling.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.360
And then through a
fortunate series of events,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.220
I ended up working on the
dark robotics challenge

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.940
and then ended up in Russ's lab.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:33.590
I came to MIT after my
PhD at Boston University.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.170
I did my PhD in robotics.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.720
And before I did robotics,
I actually studied pigeons

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.390
and how they fly and
their dynamics of flights.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.600
And so pigeons is the
way I got into robotics,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.370
by looking at the
dynamics of them

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.010
and then wondering what could
happen if you could also

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:50.750
control those systems.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:54.320
I finished my undergrad and
my masters back in 2011.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.430
And then I was working
in industry for two years

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:02.760
at Johns Hopkins applied
physics lab, which was fun,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.112
but it was more development.

00:01:04.112 --> 00:01:05.570
And I really wanted
to do research.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:08.040
I really wanted to
go back for my PhD.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.400
So I worked on a few
research projects

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.000
and I applied for
my PhD a few places.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.140
Got accepted to a few.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.540
Got rejected from a lot.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.480
And I wound up joining everybody
here at MIT for my PhD.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.040
So I did my undergrad here, and
I really liked the community.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.980
And especially I did a UROP,
the undergraduate research

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.570
with Daniella.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.820
So I enjoyed that and
I was happy to stay on

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.170
as a grad student.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.370
I won a scholarship
for my research

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:40.750
being one of the grand
challenges in engineering

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.640
and a PhD candidate at the
University of Cambridge.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.930
And this year, I'm a Charles
M. Vest scholar at MIT.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.060
I had mentioned to an
undergrad professor

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.900
that I was thinking about
maybe applying to PhD programs.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.690
And she said, you
know, you should do it.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:57.900
You should definitely do it.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.608
You know, who cares
if you get it or not?

00:01:59.608 --> 00:02:00.720
You should definitely try.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.650
And I think that little push
that someone believing in me

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.760
was what I needed to get
over my fear and apply to MIT

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.460
and that is how I got in.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.710
I came here as an undergrad and
I came to the admittance day

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:14.210
weekend.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.190
And it was a lot different
than all of the other colleges

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.570
I had been to and everyone I
met was really interesting.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.810
So I sort of just
decided to come.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.780
And then once I was here, it
became really hard to leave.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.220
So I'm still here.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

